Title:
ETW Providers Docs

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This repository collects and generates documentation for Windows Event Tracing for Windows (ETW) providers that are poorly documented by Microsoft. It includes dumped/recreated provider manifests for multiple Windows builds (e.g., Windows 7 7600 and Windows 10 17134/18990/10240) to make provider events, keywords, and schemas easier to understand and consume. The approach leverages Windows TDH APIs (tdh.h) to enumerate provider metadata and, for manifest-based providers, reconstructs manifests similarly to PerfView/TraceEvent techniques (noting these are reconstructed, not original compiled manifests). For non-manifest providers, it currently focuses on dumping keywords, with the idea that event schemas could be inferred by subscribing and observing emitted events. The repo also contains reverse-engineered manifests for specific high-value providers (e.g., kernel audit/API call related) derived from symbols or found in Microsoft open-source sources. It’s useful for red teamers, malware researchers, and defenders building ETW-based telemetry, detections, or understanding what EDRs can observe.

Technical Focus:
- ETW provider enumeration and metadata extraction (TDH / tdh.h)
- Manifest-based vs non-manifest ETW providers
- Manifest reconstruction via PerfView/TraceEvent methodology
- Reverse engineering ETW providers using Windows symbols (ntoskrnl)
- Cross-version provider/manifest differences across Windows builds

Use Cases:
- Identify high-signal ETW providers/events for detection engineering
- Build custom ETW collectors/parsers with accurate event schemas
- Map EDR/telemetry visibility and potential blind spots by provider coverage
- Research kernel/userland audit providers (e.g., API call auditing) for threat hunting
- Compare provider manifests across Windows versions for compatibility/testing

Keywords:
ETW, Event Tracing for Windows, TDH, tdh.h, TraceEvent, PerfView, provider manifest, manifest reconstruction, Windows telemetry, Microsoft-Windows-Kernel-Audit-API-Calls, Microsoft-Windows-Kernel-General, ntoskrnl symbols, keyword masks, event schema, Windows 7 7600, Windows 10 17134, Windows 10 18990, reverse engineering